--- store-size cost ---

--- Cost measurement (item 5 of the original reopening ruling, applies to the whole lineage layer including
    Gap 2's own two-pass Rust scan): a full from-clean lineage-layer build on the real corpus (freshness.run,
    layer="lineage", into its own fresh store) takes ~96s wall and produces a 413696-byte (~404 KiB) store.db.
    Not disproportionate: the same box's semantic/embedding layer alone took ~885s in an earlier full rebuild
    this run also measured (see AGENT_RUNS/BR-AR-0019.check2-freshness-determinism.out).

--- Second, independent measurement of Gap 2's own zero-count sub-kind (HEURISTIC_RUST_CLI_DISPATCH_OUTER_ARM = 0
    on the real view): see AGENT_RUNS/BR-AR-0019.check6-gap1-cites-requirement.out item 4 (a direct git-grep for a
    bare, insufficiently-qualified nested-subcommand literal call finds none in the current real corpus).

--- Disclosed limitation (stated bound, per the reopening ruling's own "allow bounded wrapper indirection, one
    level at least, and state the bound"): rust_cli.py resolves exactly the shapes the ruling describes -- a
    CARGO_BIN_EXE helper function, a direct Command::new(<helper>()).../.args([literal,...]) chain, and ONE level
    of a test-local wrapper function taking a &[&str] parameter (MAX_WRAPPER_CHAIN = 3 hops is the actual
    implemented bound, one more than the stated minimum). It does NOT resolve a harness-OBJECT method pattern
    (`some_struct.run(&[...])`/`.ok(&[...])`), which this repository's own test suite also uses in several places
    (e.g. `tests/certification/brownfield.rs`'s `planner.ok(&["adopt", "baseline"])`) -- a materially different,
    OOP-style indirection never named in the ruling's own three described shapes. This is disclosed, not silently
    narrowed: every EXPLICITLY described shape is implemented and proven, both on fixtures and on the real view.
